Chapter 9: Explain how creating and using classes supports Object Oriented Programming and S.O.L.I.D. design principles, give specific examples.
Classes are the main building blocks of object-oriented programming, as they let you group data and methods into easy to understand units. When classes
are designed around clear purposes, they also make it a lot easier to apply SOLID: Single Responsibility, Open-Closed, Liskov Substitution, Interface 
Segregation, and Dependency Inversion. The "Single Responsibility" principle of S.O.L.I.D states that a class should have one job, for example: 
class Student:
    def __init__(self, name):
        self.name = name
    def introduce(self):
        return f"My name is {self.name}."
class StudentFile:
    def save(self, student):
        print(f"Saving {student.name} to a file.")

student = Student("Bobby")
print(student.introduce())
file = StudentFile()
file.save(student)

In these two classes, the Student represents just the student themself, but the StudentFile class dictates how the student's information is saved, so if
you want to change the saving method, you would edit StudentFile and not Student.
Open-Closed says that code should be easy to extend without changing existing code, for example:
class Animal:
    def speak(self):
        pass
class Dog(Animal):
    def speak(self):
        return "Woof!"
class Cat(Animal):
    def speak(self):
        return "Meow!"

animals = [Dog(), Cat()]
for animal in animals:
    print(animal.speak())

The code will still work even if you add new animals. Liskov Substitution states that a subclass should work when placed instead of the class itself:
class Shape:
    def area(self):
        pass
class Square(Shape):
    def __init__(self, side):
        self.side = side
    def area(self):
        return self.side * self.side

def print_area(shape):
    print(shape.area())
square = Square(4)
print_area(square)

The Square subclass works when the Shape class is expected because they have the same general behavior.
Interface Segregation says that a class should not be forced to use methods it doesn't need;
class Worker:
    def write_code(self):
        pass
    def drive_truck(self):
        pass
instead of forcing every worker to both write and drive we can use other classes to share the responsibilities, since the programmer doesn't need a
drive.truck() method and a driver doesn't need a write.code() method, which avoids redundancy.
class Programmer:
    def write_code(self):
        print("Writing code.")
class Driver:
    def drive_truck(self):
        print("Driving a truck.")

programmer = Programmer()
programmer.write_code()
driver = Driver()
driver.drive_truck()

And finally, there is the Dependency Inversion, where a class should depend on a general idea and not a specific detail:
class EmailSender:
    def send(self, message):
        print(f"Email: {message}")
class Greeting:
    def __init__(self, sender):
        self.sender = sender
    def say_hello(self):
        self.sender.send("Hello!")

email = EmailSender()
greeting = Greeting(email)
greeting.say_hello()

class TextSender:
    def send(self, message):
        print(f"Text message: {message}")

text = TextSender()
greeting = Greeting(text)
greeting.say_hello()

The Greeting only requires an object with send(), and it doesn't matter which class it uses.
